  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Massachusetts received $14B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 76,880 awards with an average award size of $182K, and obligations fell 27.4% year over year. The Department of Defense dominated buying, obligating $10.9B to Massachusetts recipients. RAYTHEON COMPANY, GENERAL ELECTRIC COMPANY, and MASSACHUSETTS INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY are among the largest vendors by obligations. Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing is the single largest industry by obligations at $3B, followed by Engineering Services at $1.6B and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Biotechnology) at $1.3B. Fiscal-year obligations show a peak at $24.2B in FY21 and a decline to $14B in FY26 to date. Current-year totals are partial because the period is fiscal year to date, so annual comparisons reflect partial-year activity for FY2026.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in Massachusetts changed this fiscal year?",
        "body": "Massachusetts received $14B in obligations in FY2026 to date, down 27.4% year over year. Award volume totaled 76,880 and the average award size was $182K. Annual obligations were $24.2B in FY21 and then moved through $17.9B in FY22, $18.3B in FY23, $17.8B in FY24, and $19.3B in FY25 before the FY26 to date total of $14B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest buyers and industries in Massachusetts federal contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $10.9B in FY2026 to date. Other top buyers include the Department of Veterans Affairs at $821M, the Department of Transportation at $707M, and the Department of Health and Human Services at $471M. The largest industries by obligations are Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $3B, Engineering Services at $1.6B, and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Biotechnology) at $1.3B."
      }
    ]
  },
